// scalper and a swing trader can run the identical setup at their own scale.
sdLeg     = input.string("Swing → Entry", "SD Measured From",
     options=["Swing → Entry","IFVG Box","Full Displacement Leg","Entry → Stop (R)"], group=grpP,
     tooltip="How long ONE standard deviation is. The ladder and the entry never move, only the spacing between the rungs.\n\nSwing → Entry (default): the manipulation leg: swing extreme to the inversion close. Day-trade scale.\n\nIFVG Box: the height of the inverted gap itself. The tightest unit on the chart, so -1 and -2 sit close to the entry. Scalping: you are taking the first push off the zone, not the day's range.\n\nFull Displacement Leg: swing high to swing low of the whole impulse that built the gap, not just the half above the entry. The widest unit. Swing trading: -4 can sit a session or more away.\n\nEntry → Stop (R): one deviation = your actual risk, so the rungs read as 1R / 2R / 2.5R / 4R. This one follows 'SL Placement' above: on IFVG it is a tight R, on Swing it is a structural R. Use it when you scale out by R rather than by structure.\n\nExample on a short entered at 28,400 with the swing high at 28,460 and a 15-point-tall gap: Swing → Entry puts -1 at 28,340; IFVG Box puts it at 28,385; Entry → Stop with an IFVG stop puts it near 28,383.\n\nWorth knowing: Entry Economy reads this ladder. A trade holds its direction until it reaches the rung set in 'Trade Is Done At', so a tighter unit here means that rung gets tagged sooner and the next setup is allowed to print sooner. If you move to IFVG Box and start seeing setups stack up again, that is why. Raise 'Trade Is Done At' to compensate.")
